<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-variables">
  <title>Storing Numbers and Strings and Using Julia Syntax</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Basic syntax of strings and numbers.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Assigning values (strings and numbers) to variables.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Simple string operations like concatenation and string length.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Expressions and operator precedence.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Comments in code.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Although scientific computing problems generally involve developing models, at the lowest level, one is
      manipulating data—generally strings and numbers.
      We begin by understanding these data types and how to store values in them.
    </p>

    <p>
      We also showcase some of Julia's syntax, which looks similar to other languages like Python.
      If you have some basic knowledge of computing, it is helpful, but no particular language background is needed.
      We will see many standard programming structures in this book, with an emphasis on those relevant to solving
      scientific computing problems.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section>
    <title>Numbers</title>

    <p>
      Not surprisingly, the most important data type in scientific computing—at least at the most atomic level—is
      numbers.
      Numbers in computation mimic those in mathematics, with some important differences.
      Julia, like most computing languages, has two main number types: integers and floating-points.
      Julia's integers are much like mathematical integers in that they store numbers like <m>0, 10, -400</m>.
      Floating-point numbers generally are approximations to real or decimal numbers, such as <c>3.14159</c> or
      <c>1.23e+08</c>; more details will be covered in <xref ref="ch-data-types"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Julia also has a rational data type for numbers commonly thought of as fractions, like <m>-\frac{2}{3}</m> or
      <m>\frac{22}{7}</m>.
      Recall that typically <m>i</m> represents <m>\sqrt{-1}</m>, the base imaginary number.
      Complex numbers like <m>2+3i</m> are also native to Julia; however, instead of <m>i</m>, Julia uses <c>im</c>.
      We will explore both rational and complex numbers in greater depth in <xref ref="ch-data-types"/> and have an
      entire chapter on complex numbers in <xref ref="ch-complex"/>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Assignment Statements and Variables</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Anything can be stored as a variable using the single equal sign, like <c>x = 6</c>.
        This is an assignment operator, which creates the number 6 and stores it under the name <c>x</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now that the variable <c>x</c> is stored, we can use it in calculations.
        For example:
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>x + 3</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        returns <c>9</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Variables in Julia, much like in other languages, are primarily sequences of alphanumeric characters as well as
        the underscore character <c>_</c>.
        Primarily, a variable needs to start with an alphabetic character or <c>_</c>, and after the first character, it
        can contain numbers.
        A variable cannot have a space in the name.
      </p>

      <p>
        Julia also allows many Unicode symbols in variable names, though not everything is supported.
        For example, all of the Greek letters are allowed, so <c>α = 45</c> is valid.
      </p>

      <p>
        To generate a Greek letter in Jupyter or the REPL, type <c>\alpha</c>, press the <kbd>TAB</kbd> key, and it will
        be converted into an <c>α</c>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
      <title>Storing Variables in a Virtual Whiteboard</title>

      <p>
        While knowing the precise details of storing variables in computer hardware isn't necessary, thinking of storage
        as writing variables and values on a whiteboard is a helpful paradigm.
        Imagine a whiteboard with a column for variable names and a column for values.
        For example, if we have:
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>x = 6</cline>
          <cline>y = -1</cline>
          <cline>z = 8.5</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        then you can think of the whiteboard as looking like:
      </p>

      <table>
        <title>Whiteboard analogy of variable storage</title>

        <tabular>
          <row>
            <cell bottom="medium">Variable</cell>
            <cell bottom="medium">Value</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>x</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>y</cell>
            <cell>-1</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>z</cell>
            <cell>8.5</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        If we evaluate the expression <c>x + 3</c>, the value of <c>x</c> is looked up, and the value 6 is substituted
        into the expression to yield <c>6 + 3</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        If we alter one of the values, like running <c>y = y + 5</c>, the expression to the right of the equals sign is
        evaluated first.
        The variable <c>y</c> is looked up as <c>-1</c>, then 5 is added to it to yield 4.
        Finally, the 4 is placed into the whiteboard, which will now look like:
      </p>

      <table>
        <title>Updated whiteboard of variables</title>

        <tabular>
          <row>
            <cell bottom="medium">Variable</cell>
            <cell bottom="medium">Value</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>x</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>y</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>z</cell>
            <cell>8.5</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        When trace-evaluating how a piece of code works, you will often find it helpful to sketch out a version of this
        whiteboard.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Strings</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In many scientific computing fields, such as data science, strings arise often, and it is important to
        understand their core behavior.
        In Julia, a string is a sequence of characters surrounded by double quotes <c>""</c>.
        For example:
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>str = "This is a string"</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        If you enter <c>typeof(str)</c>, you will see the output <c>String</c>.
        The individual components of a string are called characters, which have the type <c>Char</c> and are Unicode
        characters by default (which we will see is super helpful).
        We will see other data types in <xref ref="ch-data-types"/>, but note that type names in Julia are capitalized.
        A few other helpful things to know about strings include:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The length of a string is found using the <c>length</c> function.
              For example, <c>length(str)</c> returns <c>16</c>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              To access the first element of a string, type <c>first(str)</c>.
              The last element is found via <c>last(str)</c>, and the third character can be accessed via <c>str[3]</c>.
              In Julia, string indexing starts at 1.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              To convert other data types into strings, use the <c>string()</c> function.
              For example, <c>string(3.0)</c> returns the string <c>"3.0"</c>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
      <title>String Operations</title>

      <p>
        We have seen how to access individual elements of a string.
        Another helpful operation is concatenation—the joining of two strings.
        If we define:
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>str1 = "The tide is high "</cline>
          <cline>str2 = "and I'm having fun."</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        we can concatenate them in multiple ways.
        First, we can use the <c>*</c> operator with strings as both operands, or we can use the built-in
        <c>string()</c> function.
        Both:
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>str1 * str2</cline>
          <cline>string(str1, str2)</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        return <c>"The tide is high and I'm having fun."</c> You might find that <c>*</c> is an odd syntax choice for
        string concatenation.
        Many languages, including Java and ECMAScript (JavaScript), use <c>+</c> instead.
        Another clean way of performing concatenation is string interpolation, shown below.
      </p>

      <p>
        Another unique operation for strings is the caret <c>^</c> repetition operator.
        For example:
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>"Hip, hip, hooray! "^3</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        returns:
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>Hip, hip, hooray! Hip, hip, hooray! Hip, hip, hooray!</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection>
      <title>String Interpolation</title>

      <p>
        Mixing strings and other variables together is a common task.
        If you have a variable <c>x</c> and would like to insert its evaluated value at the end of
        <c>"The value of x is "</c>, you can use concatenation as shown above, or you can leverage string interpolation
        by placing a <c>$</c> symbol directly in front of the variable name:
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>result = "The value of x is $x"</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        The evaluated result will be <c>"The value of x is 6"</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        String interpolation provides another neat mechanism for concatenation.
        If we have strings stored in variables <c>str1</c> and <c>str2</c>, evaluating <c>"$str1$str2"</c> combines them
        seamlessly.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection>
      <title>Other String Functions</title>

      <p>
        There are many other built-in string functions used for manipulation.
        The <url href="https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/base/strings/" visual="julialang.org">string chapter</url> of
        the official Julia documentation is a great reference.
      </p>

      <p>
        To effectively parse strings, a solid understanding of regular expressions is required.
        Regular expressions provide patterns to scan text and extract key structural information.
        <xref ref="ch-regexp"/> details how to construct and use regular expressions.
        We will use these patterns to parse coordinates written in string form, and in <xref ref="ch-polynomial"/>, we
        will develop a routine to parse algebraic polynomials written entirely as strings.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Expressions</title>

    <p>
      An expression is a combination of variables, data elements (such as numbers and strings), operators (like <c>+</c>
      or <c>*</c> ), and functions (like <c>length</c> ).
      We have seen a number of expressions throughout this chapter, including:
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>x = 6</cline>
        <cline>x + 3</cline>
        <cline>str1 * str2</cline>
        <cline>length(str)</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      In short, writing functional code in Julia fundamentally consists of structuring expressions and combining them
      into more complex control routines.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sect-operator-precedence">
    <title>Operator Precedence</title>

    <p>
      When typing an expression like <c>11+2*(4+3)^3</c>, it is important to understand the order in which operations
      are evaluated.
      In mathematics, the PEMDAS mnemonic defines this hierarchy:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Parentheses</em>: Expressions inside parentheses <c>( )</c> are evaluated first.
            In the example above, <c>4+3</c> is the first operation performed.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Exponentials</em>: The <c>^</c> operator is processed next.
            This raises the evaluated sum <c>7</c> to the power of <c>3</c>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Multiplication and Division</em>: In this example, the product <c>2 * 343</c> is computed next.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Addition and Subtraction</em>: Lastly, <c>11</c> is added to the running result.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Every computing language features an extended set of operational components that follow strict precedence rules.
      For example, the assignment operator <c>=</c> has the lowest precedence of all.
      When assigning a value to a variable, all operations on the right side of the <c>=</c> sign are fully computed
      before the resulting value is bound to the variable name.
    </p>

    <p>
      Deep details on associativity and evaluation hierarchies can be found on the
      <url href="https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/manual/mathematical-operations/index.html#Operator-Precedence-and-Associativity-1" visual="docs.julialang.org">Julia documentation page on operator precedence</url>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Comments</title>

    <p>
      A comment in computer code is a sequence of characters ignored by the compiler or interpreter.
      The sole purpose of a comment is to alert a human reader to what is happening inside the algorithm.
      You may have been told to write comments so that classmates or collaborators can follow along; however, the person
      most likely to read your code in the future is you at a later date.
      Write descriptive comments to help your future self.
    </p>

    <p>
      In Julia, a comment is defined by anything to the right of a <c>#</c> (hash or pound) symbol.
      For example:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
# This calculates the area of a circle
r = 3
pi*r^2 # this is the actual formula for the area
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Both lines 1 and 3 contain comments.
      On line 1, the entire line is skipped because it starts with a <c>#</c> character.
      On line 3, everything following the exponent operator is ignored.
      Notice that line 1 uses two hash marks while line 3 uses one; this is simply a cosmetic styling choice.
      Since anything after a single symbol is a comment, everything following the first marker is safely ignored.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>